schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Peer-Vouched Verification (DeVouch Model)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A decentralized alternative to a single-authority "verified" stamp:
    trusted verifiers across the ecosystem issue on-chain vouches (worth 1,
    3, or 30 points by tier) for a project, replacing one central office's
    approval decision.
  source_traditions:
    - Giveth's project-verification system, decentralized into DeVouch
    - Web-of-trust / social-vouching models generally (not named in the source article)
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Presented in this article as the mechanic that lets a local node earn
    donor-facing rewards (GIVpower boost at 3 points, GIVbacks eligibility at
    30 points) "decided by your peers, not a gate."
  adjacent_meanings:
    - Centralized platform verification (e.g. a single company's "verified" badge)
    - KYC-based identity verification (a different trust problem — identity, not project legitimacy)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Peer-vouched verification establishes project legitimacy/reputation; it
      is not the same as Sybil-resistant identity verification (see
      governance-mechanism-design.md's treatment of proof-of-humanity tools
      like Gitcoin Passport/BrightID, a related but distinct problem).
  tensions:
    - >-
      "Decided by your peers, not a gate" is still a gate — access to
      GIVpower/GIVbacks depends on which verifiers choose to vouch, which
      concentrates influence in whoever already has standing in "reputable
      organizations in the Ethereum ecosystem."
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Framing this purely as decentralization-of-trust risks obscuring that
    new/unconnected local nodes without existing Ethereum-ecosystem
    relationships may find it just as hard to get vouched as to get
    centrally verified.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/giveth-donations.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
